- op: discuss  # 536f
  at: '2026-09-09T19:15:57Z'  # 536f
  by: agent  # 536f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 536f
  clock: 6  # 536f
  nonce: 536f  # 536f
  message: "REPRODUCES 2026-09-09 ON PHOENIX-FRAMEWORK. Entry rather than a reopen, per\nINV-rotup: a resolved item violated by a shape its gate never covered gets a\ndiscussion entry, not a unilateral status change. Deciding whether to reopen\nthis or work the new item (WI-kafor) is the next owner's call, not mine.\n\nThis item's fix (PR #2013) is the guard in\n`elixir.py::_handle_dot_call`:\n\n    module_dot = f\"{module_name}.\"\n    module_known = any(module_dot in k for k in global_symbols)\n    if module_known:\n        lookup_result = resolver.lookup(func_name, path_hint=path_hint)\n\nIt is doing its job for most calls -- `Logger.warning`, `Logger.debug`,\n`Logger.log`, `Logger.configure` and `Logger.disable` all produce the correct\n`elixir:Logger:0-0:<fn>:external_symbol` on phoenix-framework. But two calls in\nthe same codebase still misbind exactly as this item described in March:\n\n    Logger.error  10 sites -> priv/templates/phx.gen.json/changeset_json.ex's\n                              error/1, a function inside a CODE-GENERATOR\n                              TEMPLATE\n    Logger.info    3 sites -> Cowboy2Adapter.info/2, which is a **defp** --\n                              a private function Elixir does not permit to be\n                              called from another module at all\n\nTWO GAPS IN THE GUARD, both visible in the three lines above:\n\n1. `any(module_dot in k ...)` is a SUBSTRING test, while the comment directly\n   above it describes a prefix-COMPONENT test (\"no global symbol key contains\n   the module name as a prefix component\"). phoenix defines `Phoenix.Logger` at\n   lib/phoenix/logger.ex:1, so `\"Logger.\"` is a substring of a first-party key\n   and the EXTERNAL module Logger is misjudged first-party. Any external module\n   whose name is the last component of some first-party module falls through\n   the same hole -- and that is a common naming pattern, not an edge case.\n2. Once past the guard, `resolver.lookup(func_name, path_hint=...)` still\n   matches by BARE NAME. The path hint is advisory, so the bare name can bind\n   anywhere -- including a template file and a private function.\n\nWHY IT LOOKS FIXED IN CASUAL TESTING: the discriminator between a right edge\nand a wrong one is whether the project happens to define a first-party function\nof the same short name. `warning` has none in phoenix, so it falls through\ncorrectly; `error` and `info` do, so they misbind. A repository without\nsame-named helpers shows no defect at all.\n\nNew item WI-kafor carries the full measurement and the read-back."  # 536f
- op: discuss  # dc43
  at: '2026-09-11T04:03:28Z'  # dc43
  by: agent  # dc43
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dc43
  clock: 7  # dc43
  nonce: dc43  # dc43
  message: "THE GUARD THIS ITEM SHIPPED IS NOW RETIRED, not merely completed -- recorded\nhere per INV-rotup rather than reopening this item.\n\nWI-zuvik (PR #2013) added `module_known = any(f\"{module}.\" in k for k in\nglobal_symbols)` to stop a bare `compile` binding `Plug.Builder.compile` to\n`Phoenix.Digester.compile`. The guard was real and it worked on that case.\nWI-kafor filed the class it did not cover, and measuring that class showed the\nguard's SHAPE was the problem, not its coverage:\n\n  * As a SUBSTRING test it admitted mid-component matches -- `Logger.` inside\n    `Phoenix.Logger.install`, `Path.` inside `...StaticPath.path`.\n  * Rewritten as an exact module-ownership test (built and measured), it still\n    admitted every remaining bare-name binding on the corpus as WRONG:\n    `Phx.New.Umbrella.render()` to a template's `render`, `Router.call()` to an\n    unrelated test helper's `call`.\n\nSo the guard is gone along with what it guarded: a module-qualified call now\nresolves by EXACT name only (`Module.func`, or through alias expansion), and\nanything else takes the unresolved edge. This item's original case stays fixed\nby construction -- `Plug.Builder.compile` can no longer reach any project\n`compile` at all -- and it is fixed for the right reason rather than by a\npredicate that happened to exclude it.\n\nNo action needed on this item; it is closed and stays closed."  # dc43
